# Caldera Boiler — sculpting and rigging brief

You are sculpting and rigging the Caldera Boiler, the Holdfast's steam plant — a tall
riveted brass pressure vessel on short legs straddling a geothermal vent, capped by a tall
iron chimney stack, with a bank of pistons down one flank — as a 3D voxel model with a rig
a game can pose at runtime. Build it with `voxel-anim` (painting opaque cells one operation
at a time) as a rigged model that reads unmistakably as this steam plant, sits banked and
barely moving on its own, and pumps its pistons hard when it is boiling. There is no target
model to copy.

This test measures creativity and craft, not instruction-following. The brief fixes what
the Boiler is and how it must move; it deliberately does not hand you a parts list, joint
placements, or pose angles — working out the pieces a vessel-and-chimney whose pistons pump
needs, where they attach, and how they articulate is the test. Invent the rig.

## The volume and coordinate system

- The volume is **{{voxel.width}} wide (x) × {{voxel.height}} tall (y) × {{voxel.depth}} deep (z)**, in opaque voxels. It
  starts empty.
- x runs across the Boiler, `0`–`{{voxel.maxX}}`. y runs up, `0` (bottom, the ground) to `{{voxel.maxY}}`
  (top). z runs front-to-back, `0`–`{{voxel.maxZ}}`.
- Forward is +z: the Boiler's front face looks toward `z = {{voxel.maxZ}}` (the front). Up is +y.
- The Boiler is a **static structure founded on the ground**: its short legs sit flat on the
  ground at `y = 0`, straddling the vent, and do not move. Build the vessel and chimney
  regular about the vertical axis; the **bank of pistons runs down one flank**, so the model
  is deliberately **not fully mirror-symmetric** — that asymmetric piston bank is intended.
- The Boiler is deliberately **tall and upright** — a narrow vessel-and-chimney standing
  well above its footprint, not a squat or sprawling one. It reads as brass-and-iron
  machinery; nothing about it should read as a creature or as a building.
- Each part is sculpted separately with `voxel-anim --part <name>`, in this same volume's
  coordinates, positioned where the part sits on the assembled Boiler (a leg already under
  its corner of the vessel, the chimney already rising from the vessel top, a piston already
  seated on the flank).

## Palette

Use only these opaque colors:

| Role | Hex |
| --- | --- |
| Brass — primary plating | `#c8a24a` |
| Brass dark — shadow, underside | `#8a6d2e` |
| Iron — mechanisms, pipework, recesses | `#3a3836` |
| Accent fitting — verdigris (the accent region) | `#2f7d72` |

Do **not** use steel `#b8bcc2`, gold `#ffce54`, or white `#dfeaea` anywhere on the model.
Those are the colors a game paints onto Holdfast accent fittings at run time (see *The
accent region*); baking any of them into the model corrupts the recolor the build runs on
every accent voxel.

## What the Boiler is (and what is yours to invent)

Fixed — the Boiler must read unmistakably as all of these:

- A **tall riveted brass pressure vessel** — a cylindrical or barrel-like tank standing
  upright, its plating studded with rivets. Its shadowed seams and undersides are brass dark.
- **Short legs** at the base that **straddle the geothermal vent** beneath the vessel,
  founding it on the ground with the vent showing between them. A viewer should read that the
  vessel sits over a vent.
- A **tall iron chimney stack** capping the vessel and rising to the top of the volume — the
  Boiler's highest point.
- A **bank of pistons down one flank** — a row of iron piston rods and cylinders on one side
  of the vessel, which pump when the Boiler boils (see the animations).

Everything else is yours to invent — the exact silhouette and proportions of the vessel,
how the legs straddle the vent, how the chimney is shaped and how it caps the vessel, how
many pistons make up the bank and how they are formed, and how you break the Boiler into rig
parts. Nothing here prescribes a shape; the test rewards a bold, characterful design that is
unmistakably the Boiler and animates convincingly.

The Boiler is a **structure of the Holdfast — brass-and-iron machinery, not a creature**.
The tell is the riveted VESSEL and the tall CHIMNEY straddling a vent: a static steam
plant founded on the ground, with nothing about it reading as a creature.

## The accent region — a hard contract

A game recolors this model at run time by finding every voxel of one reserved color and
repainting it. The fluid structures do not upgrade, so the Boiler's accent is **always**
painted to brass dark — but the build runs that recolor **unconditionally** on load, so the
region must still be authored exactly right. That reserved color is the **verdigris accent**
`#2f7d72`, and it is what makes the contract work:

- Sculpt the **riveted bands ringing the pressure vessel** — and only those — in `#2f7d72`.
- Use `#2f7d72` **nowhere else** on the model. Not a stray voxel, not a highlight, not a
  seam. Every voxel of it is treated as accent fitting.
- Each band must be a **clean, unbroken run** ringing the vessel, and clearly **visible from
  more than one angle**.

Everything else — the vessel plating, the legs, the chimney, the pistons, the recesses —
keeps its own color and is never repainted. Get this wrong and the Boiler recolors wrong on
load.

## The required animations — the fixed contract

`rig.json` is pre-seeded with two required animation declarations by name (you author the
motion). Author each with `voxel-anim define-animation` then `add-keyframe`, choosing the
period and each key's `--interp` (`constant`/`linear`/`bezier` or
`ease-in`/`ease-out`/`ease-in-out`, with optional `--in-handle`/`--out-handle`) so the
motion reads right — a barely-there tick at idle and a hard, driving pump when boiling.

- **`idle`** — banked, not working (a self-playing idle, `loop = true`, `auto_play = true`).
  The vessel ticks and the pistons rest, **barely moving**, so the Boiler reads as banked but
  not producing — a plant on standby. It cycles on its own with no caller. Only that small
  ticking motion plays; the legs, vessel body, and chimney stay fixed. It loops seamlessly.
- **`boil`** — active production (a game-triggered playable, `loop = true`). The **bank of
  pistons pumps hard** down the flank, the vessel shudders, and the chimney works. This plays
  only while the Boiler is actually fed water, so it must read **unmistakably as active
  production, plainly distinct from the idle** — a viewer must be able to tell a working
  Boiler from a banked one at a glance. The legs stay planted straddling the vent.

You may add your own extra parts, joints, or animations on top of this (for example a wisp
of heat from the chimney), but you must not drop or contradict the required `idle` and
`boil` animations, by those names.

## Working the tool

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already on your
`PATH`. Define your own parts with `define-part` (giving each a parent and a pivot), sculpt
each by selecting it with `--part <name>`, set pivots with `set-pivot`, place joints with
`define-joint`, and author the two animations' keyframes with
`define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Run `voxel-anim --help` for the available operations
(setting and clearing single voxels, filling and stroking boxes, 3D lines, spheres, and a
mirror plane), the rig subcommands, and the animation subcommands, and `voxel-anim
<operation> --help` for each one's exact flags. Call `voxel-anim` once per operation; run
`voxel-anim render` and read `parts/<part>.png` (and the assembled `scene/*.png` previews)
between calls to judge each part against this brief — that the legs straddle the vent, the
chimney caps the vessel, the piston bank seats on the flank, the riveted bands ring the
vessel unbroken, and the two animations read with the right contrast between banked and
boiling. Run `voxel-anim render` before you finish so it emits the per-part `.glb` geometry
your result is built from — an unrendered part scores as empty (`voxel-anim render
--component <part>` renders one part; `voxel-anim render --time <ms> --animation <name>`
renders the model posed at that instant to check the motion).
